<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chiapas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chiapas is a state in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> extended its conquests south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xoconusco">Xoconusco</a>, the Pacific coastal region of modern Chiapas. The region supplied valuable tribute such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and tropical feathers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chiapas.jpg" alt="Chiapas" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thelmadatter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UsumacintaYaxchilan16.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chiapas is the southernmost state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> and, to the east and south-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, and it has a long Pacific coastline. Its climate is humid and tropical but is moderated by altitude, and several parallel mountain ranges run through the centre, with cloud forests such as that of the El Triunfo biosphere reserve. The state is among the larger in Mexico, with a territory of 74,415 km2. Its capital and largest city is Tuxtla Gutiérrez.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hunter-gatherers began to occupy the central valley around 7000 BCE, and agricultural villages appeared across the state between 1800 BCE and 300 CE. The coastal Soconusco region holds the oldest sites, including the Mokaya culture, described as the oldest ceramic culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, and Paso de la Amada, with the oldest Mesoamerican ballcourt. Later the Maya built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a>, Yaxchilán, Bonampak and Toniná. The Bonampak murals are the only Mesoamerican murals preserved for over 1,500 years.</p><p>The Spanish found Maya and non-Maya peoples, the latter dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoque">Zoques</a> and the Chiapanecas. Hernán Cortés sent tax collectors in 1522, and Luis Marín led the first military incursion in 1523. Many Tzotzils resisted fiercely in the highlands, and under Diego de Mazariegos many natives preferred suicide to submission, as at the Battle of Tepetchia, where some jumped into the Sumidero Canyon. The diocese was founded in 1538, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> was its best-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Because it lacked mineral wealth and was remote and rugged, Chiapas was spared the battles of independence.</p><p>In the late 19th century Governor Emilio Rabasa took on the regional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a> and in 1892 moved the capital from San Cristóbal de las Casas to Tuxtla. Estates grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and other cash crops, and recruiters used a debt practice called enganche that trapped workers in a kind of indentured servitude. In January 1994 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapatista-army-of-national-liberation">Zapatista Army of National Liberation</a> began an armed struggle, rooted in rural poverty and in opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s economic liberalisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chiapas is one of the 32 federal entities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It has one of the largest indigenous populations in the country, with twelve federally recognised ethnicities. Its rivers supply a large share of Mexican hydroelectric power, and it is a major producer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>. The marimba is its most important instrument, and its Maya sites draw visitors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chiapas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He extended the empire south to Xoconusco (Soconusco) on the Pacific coast of modern <b>Chiapas</b>, near the present-day border with Guatemala, and marked the rededication of the enlarged Templo Mayor in 1487 with a mass sacrifice reported to have lasted four days.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Federal Republic of Central America emerged from the Captaincy General, or Kingdom, of Guatemala, the Spanish jurisdiction that stretched from <b>Chiapas</b> to Costa Rica.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 January 1994, the day NAFTA came into force, the Zapatista Army of National Liberation rose in <b>Chiapas</b>; that year also saw the assassination of the PRI candidate Luis Donaldo Colosio and, in December, a peso crisis that required an international rescue led by the United States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The border with Mexico was settled by treaty in 1882, with Guatemala renouncing its claims to <b>Chiapas</b> and Soconusco, while the claim to British Honduras, today Belize, remained a long-standing grievance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Today it is held by the extant Maya peoples of Guatemala, Belize, western Honduras and the Mexican states of Tabasco, <b>Chiapas</b>, Quintana Roo, Campeche and Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/religion/maya-religion">Maya religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Their world spanned three contrasting regions: the Pacific coastal plain; the volcanic highlands of Guatemala and <b>Chiapas</b>, rich in obsidian and jade; and the lowlands, from the rainforests of the Petén in northern Guatemala to the dry limestone plain of the Yucatán Peninsula, which has almost no rivers…</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
